      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The aim of this study was to investigate repeatability of cysteinyl-leukotrienes (cys-LT) measurements in nasal lavage fluid (NLF) and to determine if cys-LT levels in NLF are related to asthma severity in children. As a second outcome, we investigated if cys-LT in NLF reflect lower airway inflammation as assessed by exhaled NO measurement. To assess the repeatability of cys-LT measurements, two NLF samples were obtained from eight healthy controls 24 h apart. Sixty-nine asthmatic children (mean age; range: 12.8; 7.3-17.7 yr), which were grouped according to asthma severity were studied cross-sectionally on one occasion. Cys-LT in NLF were analyzed using a specific enzyme immunoassay, exhaled NO, and pulmonary function parameters were measured. The coefficient of repeatability for the repeated cys-LT measurements was 1.45 pg/ml. Cys-LT levels in NLF differed significantly between asthma severity groups (p < 0.001): mild intermittent: [median (IQR)] 6.88 pg/ml (2.00-27.87); mild persistent: 21.09 pg/ml (4.50-84.67); and moderate persistent asthmatics: 36.41 pg/ml (11.03-118.40). Concentration of cys-LT in NLF and exhaled NO was positively correlated (r = 0.85; p < 0.001). In conclusion, concentration of cys-LT in NLF correlates with asthma severity in children and is related to lower airway inflammation."},
